WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.230
Every year, we held one

2
00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:03.710
cyber safety assembly.

3
00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:05.930
The pupils loved it.

4
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.080
A month later, one of them sent her

5
00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:10.640
one-time code to a stranger

6
00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:12.400
pretending to be a game moderator.

7
00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:16.570
Today: how to teach cyber safety little

8
00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:19.130
and often, across subjects and year

9
00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:19.390
groups.

10
00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:22.550
One talk a year is forgotten.

11
00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.940
Short activities, every term, in

12
00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:28.000
many subjects, become habits.

13
00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:30.780
Ten minutes in maths, ten in

14
00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.500
languages, ten in science: it adds up,

15
00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:35.900
and nobody needs a whole new lesson.

16
00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:38.680
Maths can count password

17
00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:41.240
combinations, or crack a secret code.

18
00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:44.310
Languages can study the wording of a scam.

19
00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:46.850
Science can show how messages

20
00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:48.530
travel across a network.

21
00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.980
Social studies can talk about rights and

22
00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:54.620
laws, and art can design the posters.

23
00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:57.820
Match the message to the age.

24
00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:00.740
Younger pupils learn three things:

25
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.380
keep private things private, ask

26
00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:05.180
an adult, and be kind.

27
00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:07.610
Older pupils add privacy

28
00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:10.730
settings, two-step sign-in, scams

29
00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:12.310
and their digital footprint.

30
00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:14.750
It’s like road safety.

31
00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:17.160
We don’t teach it once to

32
00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:19.580
five-year-olds and stop; we repeat

33
00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:21.860
it, with more detail, as children

34
00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:23.780
start crossing bigger roads alone.

35
00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:25.640
Quick check!

36
00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:28.650
Which plan will really change

37
00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:30.530
what our pupils do online?

38
00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:33.810
Short activities, in many

39
00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:35.290
subjects, every term.

40
00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.300
Pupils meet the same idea in different

41
00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:41.220
places and practise it, and that is

42
00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.240
what turns knowing into doing.

43
00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:46.530
Sometimes a pupil will tell you

44
00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:48.930
about bullying, threats, or

45
00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:50.530
someone asking for photos.

46
00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:53.420
Listen calmly, believe

47
00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:55.320
them, and don’t blame.

48
00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:59.460
Never ask to see or forward images, but

49
00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:01.060
ask them to keep the messages.

50
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.160
Tell your safeguarding lead the same day,

51
00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:07.060
and if anyone is demanding money or more

52
00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:09.760
images, the answer is never to pay.

53
00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:12.730
Parents are part of the plan.

54
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.800
Share one short tip a month in the

55
00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:18.060
official parents’ group: how to set a

56
00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.180
screen lock, what a scam looks like,

57
00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:22.280
why codes are never shared.

58
00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:25.330
Keep it short and kind, in the

59
00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:26.750
languages families speak.

60
00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:28.510
Your turn.

61
00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:31.280
A pupil tells you someone online

62
00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:33.160
is threatening to share a photo

63
00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:34.580
of her unless she pays.

64
00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:37.010
Should you ask her to send you

65
00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:38.530
the photo, as evidence?

66
00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:42.750
No: never ask for or forward

67
00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:45.550
images; listen, and tell your

68
00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:47.530
safeguarding lead the same day.

69
00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:50.710
Remember: little and often, in

70
00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:53.070
every subject, matched to age,

71
00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:54.770
with parents in the loop.

72
00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:57.910
Try it in class tomorrow: ten

73
00:02:57.910 --> 00:02:59.310
minutes of secret codes.

74
00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:02.200
Pupils hide a short message by

75
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:04.800
shifting each letter and swap with a

76
00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:07.220
partner to decode it, then crack a

77
00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:09.000
longer one by counting letters.
